  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The Dodgers came to Los Angeles with a 0-4 scoreline against Ray's 4.2 innings and no answer.",
    "what_this_means": "They never broke through. Ten strikeouts and six hits is not a lineup. It's an audience. Ray gave them a chance to stay in it, and the bullpen held: Peguero threw 1.1 clean, Beck two scoreless. None of it mattered because the offense went silent after inning two. They had runners in scoring position six times and converted zero. The third and fourth innings, the Dodgers scored twice each, and by then the game was already gone. Arraez went 2-for-3, Devers 2-for-4. Individual moments that added to nothing. This is what happens when you have a starting pitcher good enough to compete and an offense that decides not to show up. The formula doesn't hold when one side goes missing entirely.",
    "what_to_watch": "Emmet Sheehan has a 4.79 ERA coming into tonight's rematch. The Dodgers will start the same pitcher who just held them off the board. The Giants need a lineup that actually swings, not walks to the stadium.",
